cast":false,"url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-19","committee":"Audit and Resources Committee","time":"6:15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-22","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-12","committee":"Policy and Performance Scrutiny Panel","time":"6:45 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-16","committee":"Audit and Resources Committee","time":"6:15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-06-22","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6:30 pm","status":"New; on the rise of the Administration Committee","webcast":false,"url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-09","committee":"Policy and Performance Scrutiny Panel","time":"6:45 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-13","committee":"Audit and Resources Committee","time":"6:15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-16","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-20","committee":"Council","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Local Government Reorganisation creating resource backfill requirements; \u00a31.7M reserve committed with further staffing pressures anticipated","lead":"A structural reorganisation event creates both direct costs and indirect service delivery risk if backfill is required; pre-commitment of 44% of the LGR reserve leaves limited buffer for unanticipated transition costs.","iso":"2026-07-16","paper_title":"Corporate Finance and Performance Report Q4 2025-26","paper_url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50043553/50043553.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"One Horton Heath capital project significantly reprofiled with slippage into future years","lead":"Slippage on a major council-owned development scheme signals either market conditions (housing demand weakness) or project delivery risk; impacts capital financing profile and debt repayment assumptions.","iso":"2026-07-16","paper_title":"Corporate Finance and Performance Report Q4 2025-26","paper_url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50043553/50043553.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Statutory compliance remediation required unplanned spend of \u00a3438K across operational and housing assets","lead":"Unplanned compliance spend of \u00a3438K indicates prior control deficiency and creates uncertainty about whether full compliance picture is now visible; regulatory failures could trigger enforcement action.","iso":"2026-07-16","paper_title":"Corporate Finance and Performance Report Q4 2025-26","paper_url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50043553/50043553.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Corporate Finance and Performance Report Q4 2025-26","paper_url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50043553/50043553.pdf","headline":"Local Government Reorganisation creating resource backfill requirements; \u00a31.7M reserve committed with further staffing pressures anticipated","detail":"The council faces transition to unitary authority in April 2028. LGR budget of \u00a31M for 2025/26 saw \u00a3774K spent with \u00a3226K carried forward. Total LGR Reserve now \u00a31.7M, of which \u00a3750K is pre-committed to staffing for the programme team, HR, Communications and Legal over the transition period. Management acknowledges potential need for staff backfill 'to ensure continuation of service provision' and sets up monitoring process for resourcing impacts.","category":"people","matters":"A structural reorganisation event creates both direct costs and indirect service delivery risk if backfill is required; pre-commitment of 44% of the LGR reserve leaves limited buffer for unanticipated transition costs.","figures":"LGR Reserve 2025/26: \u00a31.7M total; pre-committed staffing: \u00a3750K; remaining buffer: \u00a3950K","quote":"There is potential for to be a requirement to backfill staff working directly on the project to ensure continuation of service provision. It is anticipated that pressures arising from such activity will be funded from the LGR Reserve where they cannot be managed within the service.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Corporate Finance and Performance Report Q4 2025-26","paper_url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50043553/50043553.pdf","headline":"One Horton Heath capital project significantly reprofiled with slippage into future years","detail":"A material capital scheme slipped in 2025/26 with expenditure reprofiled into future years. The paper notes 'reduced capital expenditure in the year relating to One Horton Heath, as reported in previous quarters'. Section 106 payment obligations were delayed, with slippage 'predominantly related to the reprofiling of the One Horton Heath project'. This is a council-owned housing scheme that has been a focus of cabinet attention.","category":"capital","matters":"Slippage on a major council-owned development scheme signals either market conditions (housing demand weakness) or project delivery risk; impacts capital financing profile and debt repayment assumptions.","figures":"Capital expenditure 2025/26: \u00a344M total; significant portion of slippage related to One Horton Heath","quote":"Slippage on the Community Investment Programme in 2025/26 predominantly related to the reprofiling of the One Horton Heath project, with the impact being that payments for Section 106 obligations needed to be made later than initially envisaged.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Corporate Finance and Performance Report Q4 2025-26","paper_url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50043553/50043553.pdf","headline":"Statutory compliance remediation required unplanned spend of \u00a3438K across operational and housing assets","detail":"A comprehensive statutory compliance review identified fire, electrical, and safety regulation failures. Operational property assets required \u00a3185K immediate remediation to meet Fire Safety Order 2005, Gas Safety Regulation 1998, HSAWA, Building Regulations, Electricity at Work Regulations, and Lifting Equipment Regulations. Housing portfolio required additional \u00a3253K for fire/electrical safety and damp/mould remediation to comply with Awaab's law. These were unplanned costs triggered by the review.","category":"governance","matters":"Unplanned compliance spend of \u00a3438K indicates prior control deficiency and creates uncertainty about whether full compliance picture is now visible; regulatory failures could trigger enforcement action.","figures":"Operational property remediation: \u00a3185K; housing portfolio compliance: \u00a3253K; total: \u00a3438K","quote":"A comprehensive statutory compliance review across the year identified improvements for fire and electrical safety across the Council's operational property portfolio. There were remediation works required to remove unforeseen non-compliance with a spend of \u00a3185,000","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Corporate Finance and Performance Report Q4 2025-26","paper_url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50043553/50043553.pdf","headline":"Housing Benefit subsidy deficit worsening with no control over costs; trend expected to deteriorate further","detail":"Housing Benefit and Council Tax Support overspent by \u00a3307K in 2025/26 on demand-led services. The council identifies a structural problem: Universal Credit migration has shifted case mix toward high-cost rent officer determination cases that attract minimal subsidy. Individual claims can exceed \u00a350K per year with only \u00a310K subsidy. The finance report explicitly states this trend is expected to worsen and will require further budget growth.","category":"demand","matters":"A growing, uncontrollable demand-led pressure with explicit expectation of deterioration signals structural budget risk that cannot be managed through service efficiency.","figures":"Housing Benefit overspend 2025/26: \u00a3307K; individual claims: up to \u00a350K/year with \u00a310K subsidy","quote":"This trend of increasingly complex and costly cases is expected to worsen in future years and likely to require further growth.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Corporate Finance and Performance Report Q4 2025-26","paper_url":"https://eastleigh.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s50043553/50043553.pdf","headline":"Investment Property Reserve will be exhausted in 2027/28, forcing \u00a31.4M General Fund impact","detail":"The council's Investment Property Reserve is being drawn down faster than planned due to void costs and lease renewal incentives. A net draw of \u00a33.6M occurred in 2025/26 (\u00a32.8M budgeted). Forecast balances show the reserve declining from \u00a33.162M (2025/26) to \u00a3452K (2026/27) to \u00a30 (2027/28), at which point a \u00a31.4M annual cost will hit the General Fund. The primary driver is Wessex House closure in February 2025 and incentives needed to secure lease renewals.","category":"reserves","matters":"A council burning through a major asset reserve within 18 months signals deteriorating income-generating capacity and upcoming structural deficit pressures, which directly affects debt serviceability.","figures":"Investment Property Reserve: \u00a33.162M (2025/26), \u00a3452K (2026/27), \u00a30 (2027/28); General Fund impact 2027/28: \u00a31.4M; net draw 2025/26: \u00a33.6M (vs \u00a32.8M budgeted)","quote":"This shows that the Investment Property Reserve is forecast to be exhausted during 2027/28 with future costs needing to be borne by the General Fund.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-16"},"name":"Eastleigh","findings_total":58,"news_drafts_total":3}}